  "3917eccd40c0801080b8ed9b59024d33": {
    "id": "3917eccd40c0801080b8ed9b59024d33",
    "title": "Follow Up Structure",
    "body": "**Goal:** Give you the entire SOP so you can follow up with every qualified lead correctly, at the right time, in the right tone, without leads slipping through the cracks.\n\nIt is super important to use different follow ups for different areas of the conversation. You MUST follow up with every qualified lead, no matter where in the conversation the lead drops off.\n\nMessages a **SETTER** will be sending will be in **BLUE**. Messages a **LEAD** sends will be in **GREEN**.\n\n### Why Are Follow Ups Important?\n\n- Follow ups are the number one way to convert leads through the DM’s.\n- On average it takes a lead around 7 different touch points in the DMs in order to book in.\n- Without follow ups leads are not being warmed up, missing out on them means you only have one shot to book a lead in.\n- When you use follow ups properly you are able to actively push leads through the sales process and accelerate the effect your marketing has by building a relationship over multiple conversations.\n- On top of this follow ups are the number one leaver you can use to maintain a strong pipeline of leads in times of slower lead flow.\n\n---\n\n### The Only Two Types Of Follow Up\n\nThe biggest issue I find is that business owners say follow ups feel disingenuous with people when saying ‘say let me know’ or ‘yo’. They feel like they are begging the lead to respond and lose all authority.’\n\nHow do we solve this? Simple, we give the lead a clear reason about why we are sending them a message and we give them a clear incentive to respond.\n\n1. **Value Based follow ups**\n\nThis follow up is ideally sent when you have already discussed the lead's problem.\n\n*EG: yoo just recorded a yt video and remembered you were having some issues with (PAIN), you should defo check this out, I go into detail on XYZ*\n\n1. **Rapport Based**\n\nThis follow up should be sent if the convo dies off in the rapport stage, or if a lead doesn't reply after a salesy question or heavy value question.\n\n*EG: is this Italy? thinking of taking my girl on a trip to amalfi*\n\n---\n\n### How Can I Manage My Setter’s Follow Ups\n\n#### 1. Tracking\n\n- On your sales dashboard it is vital to review lead tracker on team meetings in order to keep note of the warmest leads and to make sure your setter is filling in the table to build sa proper pipeline.\n\n![Screenshot 2026-07-02 at 17.51.36.png](/acquisition/Screenshot_2026-07-02_at_17.51.36.png)\n\n#### 2. KPI’s + EOD Reporting\n\n- It is important to set standards on the expected amount of follow ups per day with your setter, this can be reviewed through their end of day reports.\n\n#### **3. Quality Tracking**\n\n- Volume alone doesn't tell you if the follow ups are actually good. When reviewing the lead tracker, spot check a sample of the follow ups sent and check:\n    - Was this Value Based or Rapport Based, and did it match the stage the lead dropped off at?\n    - Did it sound natural, or copy-pasted from the template word for word?\n- A setter can hit their follow up quota while still sending the wrong type every time - quality checks catch this before it becomes a bad habit across the whole pipeline.\n\n---\n\n### Checklist Before Sending A Follow Up\n\n- Is this Value Based or Rapport Based, and does it match where the lead dropped off?\n    \n    [unchecked](data:image/png;base64,)\n    \n- Have I checked the lead's usual response time/pattern instead of just using the default timestamp?\n    \n    [unchecked](data:image/png;base64,)\n    \n- Does the message sound natural, not copy-pasted?\n    \n    [unchecked](data:image/png;base64,)",
    "groups": [],
    "embeds": []
  },
